# This repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so tracked files
# are checked out with CRLF by default. That is fine for sources the toolchain
# reads, but fatal for a shell script the MSYS/Git-bash `bash` actually
# executes: every trailing \r ends up inside the token it terminates, so
# `TIMEOUT="${TIMEOUT:-300}"` yields "300\r" and the first arithmetic
# comparison against it fails.
#
# The Hibernate suite runner and its per-class override table are tracked
# specifically so they cannot silently vanish (see
# apps/hib-suite-runner/class-overrides.tsv's header). Pin them to LF so the
# copy git materialises is the copy that runs.
#
# Pinned for EVERY tracked shell script, not just the two noticed first. The
# hazard is a property of "git materialises this file and bash executes it", so
# an allow-list of individual paths only waits for the next script to be added
# — `probes/*.sh`, including the pre-existing hib-mapresize reproducer, was
# already exposed while this file listed just the two below.
*.sh text eol=lf
apps/hib-suite-runner/run-hib.sh text eol=lf
apps/hib-suite-runner/class-overrides.tsv text eol=lf
apps/hib-suite-runner/known-benign-aborts.tsv text eol=lf

# The strict-corpus baselines are read line-by-line by
# scripts/jdk-only-strict-probes.sh under bash. A trailing CR ends up inside
# the comparison key, so every baselined entry reads as absent and every
# observed one reads as new -- the ratchet would fire on all 28 at once, on
# Windows only. Same hazard, same fix, as the shell scripts above.
scripts/baselines/*.txt text eol=lf

# Same hazard for the JDK surface baselines. `scripts/jdk-baseline/generate.py`
# writes them with an explicit '\n' so the file is byte-identical on every
# platform, and its `--check` mode diffs the working tree against a fresh
# generation. With core.autocrlf=true and no pin here, the checked-out copy is
# CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux while the generator's output is always LF, so
# the check would answer differently on the two hosts for a difference that is
# not in the data. Both readers trim_end() anyway; this makes the bytes match.
#
# Measured before this line existed: `git ls-files --eol scripts/baselines/`
# reports `i/lf w/crlf` for every unpinned neighbour in this directory
# (`jdk-only-kind-map-25-linux.tsv`, `jdk-only-dead-everywhere.tsv`, …) and
# `i/lf w/lf attr/text eol=lf` for the one file the stanza above pins. The
# jdk25-*.tsv are LF in the working tree today only because they were just
# written by the generator and have not yet been through a checkout; the first
# clone on Windows is where they would flip.
scripts/baselines/jdk25-*.tsv text eol=lf

# Rust sources, pinned to LF for the same class of reason as the scripts above,
# but a subtler failure. Several tests in native-builtins read
# native-builtins/src/lib.rs *as text* and match 
-anchored patterns against
# it -- counting registration sites, locating a column-0 closing brace after a
# named fn. Any tool that rewrites a .rs file with CRLF endings (a Python
# read_text/write_text round trip on Windows does exactly that) breaks four of
# those tests at once, and every failure message points at registration
# bookkeeping rather than at line endings.
*.rs text eol=lf
# CUDA sources and the PTX generated from them. nvcc emits CRLF on Windows,
# and vm/src/runtime/kernels/mod.rs reads BOTH files back as text to check
# they have not drifted apart (entry points, target arch, the TILE constant).
# Left unpinned, a checkout on another platform changes their bytes and those
# tests start failing for a reason that has nothing to do with the kernels --
# which is exactly what the *.rs pin above already exists to prevent.
*.cu  text eol=lf
*.ptx text eol=lf

# The same reasoning for the other tracked text the toolchain parses rather
# than merely compiles.
*.toml text eol=lf
*.tsv  text eol=lf
